\documentclass{article}
\title{Point-of-Care Ultrasound in Rural Triage: A Pilot Study}
\author{Rural Health Imaging Consortium}
\date{February 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Rural clinics often lack access to formal radiology services.
Point-of-care ultrasound can close part of that gap.
Handheld devices have become affordable for small clinics.
Their image quality in triage settings remains poorly documented.
This pilot study evaluated one handheld device across four clinics.
Each clinic used the device for twelve weeks.
Nurses with limited scanning experience performed the examinations.
A telemedicine link provided remote supervision when needed.
\section{Methods}
Four rural clinics joined the pilot between March and June.
Each clinic received the same handheld device and protocol.
Nurses completed a two-day training course before the study.
Scans were reviewed remotely within twenty-four hours.
Image quality was scored on a five-point scale by two radiologists.
Disagreements were resolved in a joint reading session.
The first figure shows the device used in all four clinics.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{The handheld ultrasound device used in all four clinics.}
\label{fig:device}
\end{figure}
Patient consent covered scanning, storage, and remote review.
The protocol was approved by the consortium ethics board.
\section{Results}
A total of 412 scans were performed during the pilot.
Abdominal scans made up the largest share.
The second figure summarizes B-mode image quality by clinic.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{B-mode image quality scores by clinic.}
\label{fig:bmode}
\end{figure}
Doppler examinations succeeded in most attempted cases.
The third figure reports Doppler signal quality across patient positions.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Doppler signal quality across patient positions.}
\label{fig:doppler}
\end{figure}
A thyroid substudy ran at two of the four clinics.
The fourth figure shows strain measurements for that cohort.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Strain measurements for the thyroid cohort.}
\label{fig:strain}
\end{figure}
Follow-up imaging was scheduled for all abnormal findings.
The fifth figure tracks follow-up completion at thirty days.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Follow-up completion rates at thirty days.}
\label{fig:followup}
\end{figure}
Clinics with prior scanning experience scored highest.
Remote review changed the initial read in 9 percent of cases.
\section{Discussion}
Handheld ultrasound proved workable in routine rural triage.
Training time was the main bottleneck reported by nurses.
Image quality improved steadily over the twelve weeks.
Telemedicine supervision was used less than expected.
The consortium will extend the pilot to eight more clinics.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{1.5cm}\rule{1.5cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Clinic network emblem.}
\end{figure}
\section{Conclusion}
Point-of-care ultrasound is feasible in nurse-led rural triage.
Structured training and remote review support safe adoption.
A larger rollout will test sustainability over a full year.
\end{document}

